/**
 * @page topic_server_protocol Server ACIP Handling
 * @ingroup module_server
 * @brief How server callbacks turn validated ACIP packets into application work.
 *
 * The server receives packets through `network_connection_t`. Its connection
 * callback handles negotiation, then passes READY traffic to the shared ACIP
 * handlers with the connection's `acip_transport_t` and `client_info_t` context.
 *
 * @section server_protocol_receive Receive path
 *
 * The transport supplies complete packet boundaries. The ACIP layer checks
 * packet type, payload length, CRC, security state, and negotiated capabilities.
 * Handlers then update terminal capabilities, stream state, media buffers,
 * audio mixer sources, or discovery state. Payload pointers are borrowed for
 * the callback; queues copy or take ownership explicitly.
 *
 * @section server_protocol_media Media path
 *
 * Incoming video enters the client's media state and is available to the server
 * stream/render pipeline. Incoming PCM, batched audio, and Opus batches enter
 * the client's audio buffer and mixer. Outbound ASCII, encoded video, and mixed
 * audio use the shared ACIP send helpers and bounded queues.
 *
 * @section server_protocol_control Control and shutdown
 *
 * Ping/pong, stream controls, terminal-size updates, errors, and server state
 * stay on the reliable control path. A protocol or authentication failure stops
 * the connection; it is not silently downgraded to an unauthenticated session.
 * Closing stops producers before the connection and its transport are released.
 *
 * @see topic_server_client
 * @see topic_packet_types
 * @see topic_network
 */
